Descriptor comparison on LikeOS.

os_same_file_description() answers whether two descriptors name one open
file description.  The Intel driver duplicates the device descriptor it
is given (one copy for the buffer manager, one for the screen) and asks
this before exporting a buffer handle to the screen's copy: the same
description means the buffer's own handle is valid there and nothing
else is needed.

The function only knew how to ask on the system it was written for; on
LikeOS it returned "cannot tell", and the driver then took the other
path -- exported the buffer as a dma-buf and imported it again through
the screen's descriptor.  The kernel hands back the handle the buffer
already has (one handle per object per description), which the driver
recorded as a second, separately owned handle and closed a second time
when the buffer was freed.  Freed handle numbers are reused at once, so
the second close usually destroyed whatever buffer had taken the number
in between: the device faulted on addresses that no longer existed, or a
submission named a handle that was gone and the driver aborted.  When
the number had not yet been reused the kernel refused the close, which
is the GEM_CLOSE EINVAL seen once per client.

LikeOS answers the question through fcntl(F_DUPFD_QUERY), the same
interface the function already prefers; it simply was not consulted for
this system.

diff -Nur -x .likeos-build -x '*.o' -x '.likeos-*' a/src/util/os_file.c b/src/util/os_file.c
--- a/src/util/os_file.c
+++ b/src/util/os_file.c
@@ -243,7 +243,7 @@
    if (fd1 == fd2)
       return 0;
 
-#if DETECT_OS_LINUX
+#if DETECT_OS_LINUX || DETECT_OS_LIKEOS
    /* Use F_DUPFD_QUERY if available. */
    int r = fcntl(fd1, F_DUPFD_QUERY, fd2);
 
